# If a file is set here, then PRs will require that team's approval to get that code merged.

# WE DO NOT USE CODEOWNERS FOR MANAGING ORDINARY RISK.

# Risk of outages, incidents, and operational difficulty
# are not sufficient reason to add a region of the codebase
# to the CODEOWNERS file. Handle these by de-risking your
# code, or by modifying CI to suit your products needs.

# Adding entries to the codeowners file is an anti-social
# and anti-posthog-values thing to do. It must have extraordinary
# justification. If you are not _certain_ there is _no other way_
# to manage the risk you're thinking about, do not add an entry.

# team-security's required reviews are not in this file. The `master` branch ruleset requires
# them (Settings > Rules > Rulesets > master), so an edit to this file cannot remove them. They
# cover the CI security scans, the pull_request_target workflows and their scripts, the
# authentication code, the Content Security Policy and the dev sandbox.

/.github/CODEOWNERS @PostHog/team-devex

# ClickHouse team owns Clickhouse migrations
posthog/clickhouse/migrations/** @PostHog/clickhouse

# HogQL team owns HogQL changes
posthog/hogql/** @PostHog/hogql
# ...except per-table schema definitions, which product teams add and maintain for their own features
posthog/hogql/database/schema/**
# ...and the generated schema snapshot, which changes when those definitions are regenerated
posthog/hogql/database/test/__snapshots__/test_database.ambr
# ...and documentation, which does not change HogQL behavior
posthog/hogql/**/*.md

# The distributed-ownership resolver that the auto-assign-reviewers workflow executes on its
# pull_request_target event (via `python -m owners_yaml`), with packages/owners-yaml
# as the privileged sys.path root. Anything importable placed in that tree can
# shadow stdlib/pyyaml for the privileged process — top-level modules (yaml.py),
# shadow packages (yaml/__init__.py), and extension modules (yaml.so, *.pyd)
# alike — so the whole directory is gated rather than enumerating suffixes.
# DevEx maintains the resolver and reviews it with that risk in mind.
packages/owners-yaml/** @PostHog/team-devex

# Migration safety exceptions disable blocking checks and need independent DevEx approval.
/posthog/management/migration_analysis/hot_table_acknowledged_migrations.txt @PostHog/team-devex
/posthog/management/migration_analysis/atomic_false_acknowledged_migrations.txt @PostHog/team-devex
/.github/scripts/migration-deletion-allowlist.txt @PostHog/team-devex

# Ingestion team owns persons migrations
rust/persons_migrations/** @PostHog/team-ingestion

# The MCP store catalog is a fleet-wide trust decision, not ordinary code: a merged entry
# becomes (probe permitting) an active server template in every environment on the next
# deploy — installable by all tenants and feeding tool results into their LLM agents. The
# probe only verifies liveness, so PR review is the vendor-identity gate.
products/mcp_store/backend/catalog.py @PostHog/team-context-and-mcp

# Adding a line here exempts a product from isolation, so the full Django backend suite
# keeps running on every change it makes — a cost paid by everyone, not just its team.
# `hogli product:bootstrap` scaffolds a sealed product, so a new one should never need an
# entry. Removals are the mechanical result of a product sealing and are a rubber stamp.
products/isolation_baseline.txt @PostHog/team-devex

# Agent instructions and the PR description format apply to every PR in the repo. They have
# changed back and forth many times, and each change undid an earlier lesson that the author
# could not see, so DevEx reviews them with that history in mind. Nested AGENTS.md files stay
# with the teams that own their directories.
/AGENTS.md @PostHog/team-devex
/CLAUDE.md @PostHog/team-devex
/.agents/agent-automation.md @PostHog/team-devex
/.agents/architecture.md @PostHog/team-devex
